WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.171
This chapter is about the arterial to mixed venous oxygen content difference, written C a minus v O2.

2
00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:19.489
It is the difference between the oxygen in arterial blood and the oxygen left in mixed venous blood, so it reflects how much oxygen the tissues used.

3
00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:34.300
We calculate both contents with Hess's worked example, connect the difference to the Fick equation, and then cover what widens or narrows it, and the diseases where the decks say it stays normal.

4
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:49.110
Arterial oxygen content is one point three four, times the hemoglobin, times the arterial saturation, plus point zero zero three times the PaO2.

5
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:56.188
The answer is in milliliters of oxygen per one hundred milliliters of blood.

6
00:00:56.188 --> 00:01:07.828
RCP 203 Lesson 7 gives a normal range of sixteen to twenty two, and prints the dissolved factor as point zero zero three one.

7
00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:16.489
That is the same quantity; use point zero zero three in arithmetic, as Lesson 3a and Hess do.

8
00:01:16.489 --> 00:01:27.291
Hess's resting example uses a hemoglobin of fifteen, a saturation of ninety seven percent and a PaO2 of one hundred.

9
00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:34.834
Fifteen times one point three four times point nine seven is nineteen point five.

10
00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:39.956
One hundred times point zero zero three is point three.

11
00:01:39.956 --> 00:01:42.750
Together that is about twenty.

12
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:55.117
Mixed venous oxygen content uses the same formula with venous values, from a mixed venous sample drawn from the pulmonary artery.

13
00:01:55.117 --> 00:02:04.880
In Hess's example, the hemoglobin is fifteen, the venous saturation seventy five percent, and the venous PO2 forty.

14
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:12.180
Fifteen times one point three four times point seven five is fifteen point zero eight.

15
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.510
Forty times point zero zero three is point one two.

16
00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.141
Together that is about fifteen.

17
00:02:19.141 --> 00:02:29.837
The difference is twenty minus fifteen, or five milliliters per one hundred milliliters, about four point six before rounding.

18
00:02:29.837 --> 00:02:38.411
That subtraction is the study guide's own, from Hess's numbers, and five matches Hess's normal value.

19
00:02:38.411 --> 00:02:53.267
One printing note: Hess's text shows the dissolved part as point zero three, but one hundred times point zero zero three is point three, and Hess's own total uses point three.

20
00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:58.235
The content difference feeds the Fick equation.

21
00:02:58.235 --> 00:03:03.854
Oxygen consumption equals the content difference times the cardiac output.

22
00:03:03.854 --> 00:03:21.243
With a difference of five milliliters per one hundred milliliters, which is fifty milliliters per liter, and a cardiac output of five liters per minute, the consumption is about two hundred fifty milliliters of oxygen per minute.

23
00:03:21.243 --> 00:03:27.242
At rest, the tissues extract about twenty five percent of the oxygen delivered.

24
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:32.507
Pause and work it out with Hess's numbers.

25
00:03:32.507 --> 00:03:34.885
The hemoglobin is fifteen.

26
00:03:34.885 --> 00:03:41.378
Arterial saturation is ninety seven percent with a PaO2 of one hundred.

27
00:03:41.378 --> 00:03:48.236
Mixed venous saturation is seventy five percent with a venous PO2 of forty.

28
00:03:48.236 --> 00:03:52.717
What is each content, and what is the difference?

29
00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:59.633
[6-second practice pause]

30
00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:14.189
Arterial content is fifteen times one point three four times point nine seven, nineteen point five, plus one hundred times point zero zero three, point three, for about twenty.

31
00:04:14.189 --> 00:04:28.246
Venous content is fifteen times one point three four times point seven five, fifteen point zero eight, plus forty times point zero zero three, point one two, for about fifteen.

32
00:04:28.246 --> 00:04:35.433
The difference is about five milliliters per one hundred milliliters, Hess's normal value.

33
00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:47.519
Hess says oxygen extraction rises, so mixed venous content falls, with exercise, congestive heart failure and severe anemia.

34
00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:55.362
Extraction falls, so venous content rises, when cardiac output rises greatly, as in sepsis.

35
00:04:55.362 --> 00:05:11.735
Hess does not state the effect on the content difference itself, but since the difference is arterial minus venous content, a lower venous content at an unchanged arterial content widens it.

36
00:05:11.735 --> 00:05:15.269
That step is the study guide's inference.

37
00:05:15.269 --> 00:05:24.576
A venous PO2 below thirty five in a critically ill patient suggests increased extraction and tissue hypoxia.

38
00:05:24.576 --> 00:05:36.037
Hess gives normal mixed venous values of thirty five to forty five for the PO2 and sixty five to seventy five percent for saturation.

39
00:05:36.037 --> 00:05:45.258
The RCP 105 deck gives seventy five percent, with a range of sixty eight to seventy seven, so use the deck.

40
00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:51.747
The RCP 103 disease decks add one finding and one trap.

41
00:05:51.747 --> 00:06:00.616
In a large pleural effusion, the content difference increases, because the tissues extract more.

42
00:06:00.616 --> 00:06:19.461
But in ARDS, interstitial lung disease, lung cancer, pulmonary embolism and severe obstructive sleep apnea, the decks say the content difference stays normal, even where the oxygen extraction ratio rises.

43
00:06:19.461 --> 00:06:23.987
The trap is to call it widened in those diseases.

44
00:06:23.987 --> 00:06:24.542
Don't.

45
00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:26.805
One more.

46
00:06:26.805 --> 00:06:32.582
A patient has ARDS, and the oxygen extraction ratio has risen.

47
00:06:32.582 --> 00:06:41.433
According to the RCP 103 deck, what happens to the arterial to mixed venous content difference?

48
00:06:41.433 --> 00:06:47.117
And in which pleural condition do the decks say it increases?

49
00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:54.033
[6-second practice pause]

50
00:06:54.533 --> 00:07:02.629
In ARDS, the deck says the content difference stays normal, even though the extraction ratio rises.

51
00:07:02.629 --> 00:07:05.818
Calling it widened is exactly the trap.

52
00:07:05.818 --> 00:07:15.958
The pleural condition where the decks say it increases is a large pleural effusion, because the tissues extract more oxygen.

53
00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:19.836
Here is the chapter in brief.

54
00:07:19.836 --> 00:07:29.626
Oxygen content is one point three four times hemoglobin times saturation, plus point zero zero three times the PO2.

55
00:07:29.626 --> 00:07:46.823
Arterial content is normally sixteen to twenty two, Hess's example gives about twenty for arterial and fifteen for mixed venous, and the difference is about five milliliters per one hundred milliliters.

56
00:07:46.823 --> 00:07:56.699
That difference times the cardiac output gives the oxygen consumption, about two hundred fifty milliliters a minute.

57
00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:04.105
Extraction rises with exercise, heart failure and severe anemia, and falls with sepsis.

58
00:08:04.105 --> 00:08:14.492
A large effusion increases the difference, but in ARDS, ILD, lung cancer, embolism and severe sleep apnea it stays normal.
